*Aim:- Write a C program with a buffer overflow vulnerability, exploiting the vulnerability and discussing mitigation strategies*

*Theory -* A buffer overflow is a vulnerability that occurs when a program writes more data into a fixed size buffer that it can hold. The excess data may overwrite adjacent memory, causing program crashes, data corruption or unintended program execution

- gets() to fgets() limits the number of character read
- strcpy() to strncpy() allows control over the amount of data copied
- strcat() to strncat() helps prevent writing beyond the destination
- sprintf() to snprintf() limits the number of character written
- scanf("%s", ...) to scanf("%Ns", ...) limits input length

*Tools used -* C programming

*Procedure:*

*1) Displaying string and buffer size using sizeof()*
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
int main() {
 char name[10];
 strcpy(name, "Riya");
 printf("%s | Buffer Size: %zu bytes\n", name, sizeof(name));
 return 0
}
*2) Demonstration of buffer overflow using strcpy()*
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
int main() {
 char name[10];
 strcpy(name, "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOP");
 printf("Value : %s\n", name);
 return 0;
}
*3) Demonstration of buffer overflow in a structure*
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
struct StudentData {
  char buffer[8];
  char buffer[8]; // [in image it says buffer twice, but logic is status]
};

int main() {
 struct StudentData data;
 strcpy(data.status, "SAFE");
 printf("BEFORE: Status = %s\n", data.status);
 strcpy(data.buffer, "AAAAAAAAAAHACKED");
 printf("AFTER: Buffer = %s\n", data.buffer);
 printf("AFTER: Status = %s\n", data.status);
 return 0;
}
*4) Restricted input using scanf()*
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
 char username[10];
 printf("Enter username: ");
 scanf("%9s", username);
 printf("username: %s\n", username);
 return 0;
}
*5) Safe string input using fgets()*
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
 char username[10];
 printf("Enter username: ");
 fgets(username, sizeof(username), stdin);
 printf("username: %s", username);
 return 0;
}
*6) Demonstration of memory addresses and buffer overflow*
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
struct StudentData {
  char buffer[8];
  char status[8];
};

int main() {
 struct StudentData data;
 strcpy(data.status, "SAFE");
 printf("Address of buffer: %p\n", (void*)data.buffer);
 printf("Address of status: %p\n", (void*)data.status);
 strcpy(data.buffer, "AAAAAAAAAAHACKED");
 printf("After overflow, status = %s\n", data.status);
 return 0;
}
*7) Safe string handling using snprintf()*
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
int main() {
 char buffer[10];
 snprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), "%s", "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRS TUVWXYZ");
 printf("Stored value: %s\n", buffer);
 return 0;
}
*Conclusion -* The experiment helps us in understanding buffer handling and string operations in C. It demonstrated buffer overflow and different techniques for safe input and string handling
